# =============================================================================
# .dockerignore: what stays out of the model image
# =============================================================================
#
# WHERE THIS FILE BELONGS
#   At the top of the model repository, next to the folder docker_setup, under the
#   name ".dockerignore" (with the dot, without any extension).
#
# WHAT IT DOES
#   "docker build" first sends a set of files to Docker: the "build context". The IMPACT
#   Docker GUI and docker_build_push.* use the whole repository folder as build context,
#   and Dockerfile.IMPACTncdGER copies all of it into the image. Every line below names
#   files that Docker leaves out of that set, so they never reach the image.
#   Without this file, model results, synthetic populations and the git history end up
#   in the image, and each of them is stored there several times.
#
# WHY THIS IS SAFE
#   The GUI mounts the repository folder into the container at every start, so inside
#   a running container the files on disk are used and the copy in the image is hidden.
#   The image needs the copy only while it is built: for the R package in Rpackage/,
#   for the large files listed in simulation/large_files_indx.csv, and for the three
#   start-up scripts in docker_setup/. This file excludes none of them.
#
# ASSUMPTIONS ABOUT THE REPOSITORY (check them when a model repository differs)
#   1. The results folder is "outputs" and the synthetic populations are in
#      "inputs/synthpop", both directly below the repository folder. These are the two
#      places where the GUI mounts the folders named in sim_design.yaml.
#   2. No file the image build needs lies inside these two folders: no part of the R
#      package, and no file or chunk listed in simulation/large_files_indx.csv.
#   3. The image build needs no file called .env, .Rhistory or .RData, and nothing in a
#      folder called .Rproj.user.
#
# RULES OF THIS FILE FORMAT
#   - A line that starts with "#" is a comment. A "#" later in a line is part of the
#     pattern, so comments always stand on lines of their own.
#   - A pattern is a path from the top of the repository, with "/" between folders,
#     also on Windows. A pattern without a folder part matches only at the top.
#   - "*" stands for any part of one name. "**/" at the start stands for "in any
#     folder, at any depth, including the top".
# =============================================================================

# git's own folder: the full history of the repository. It can be larger than the
# files themselves, and nothing in the image uses it (git works on the mounted folder).
.git

# Model results. "/*" leaves out everything inside the folder and keeps the empty
# folder itself, so the image has an "outputs" folder as before, only without content.
outputs/*

# Synthetic populations (large files the model creates). Same rule as for outputs.
inputs/synthpop/*

# RStudio's state of the open project: open files, the history of the console and
# similar. RStudio writes it while a person works in the repository.
**/.Rproj.user

# R's command history and saved workspace (R writes them when a session ends with
# "save workspace"). A saved workspace can be several gigabytes.
**/.Rhistory
**/.RData

# Files named .env hold private settings, for example the Docker Hub token in
# docker_setup/.env that docker_build_push.* and the GUI read. Everyone who can use
# an image can read every file in it, so such files must never be copied into one.
**/.env
